# Approval-gated batch ledger

Cargo-native Rust local application: one ledger, one transactional SortedMap,
one workflow owner and canonical typed Tasks.Wait. Direct gRPC only; trusted
single-server development network, not production or mixed-language placement.

```sh
export RBT_RUST_DATABASE_BINARY=/absolute/path/to/reboot/server/database
rbt dev run --rust-allow-insecure-database --port=12991
# separate terminal:
export RBT_RUST_URL=http://127.0.0.1:12991
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- create
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- watch
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- submit batch-001 3 11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- approve batch-001 0
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- approve batch-001 1
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- approve batch-001 2
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- wait RETURNED_UUID 5000
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- history batch-001
```

Submit keys replay the same task handle; only one active batch is accepted.
Optional final submit argument is a future UTC Unix timestamp. Counts are 1..100,
batch names bounded ASCII alphanumeric/hyphen. Approvals must arrive in order.
Each approval commits ledger state and index entry in one fresh exclusive root;
`approve-invalid` catches an invalid map range but still aborts the whole root.
Workflow steps use finite `batch-v1` iterations, saved pure reader observations,
and named writer checkpoints. No actor lease is held while awaiting approval.
`read`/`watch` show batch, count, approved, completed and submissions. Handler
entries in the normal host log make replay vs remutation inspectable.

The host creates the empty map before public readiness; public `create` then
constructs the Ledger separately. These are not an atomic two-actor constructor.
A crash leaving only the empty map is repaired by stable constructor replay.

## Declared business rejection

`submit-rejecting batch count KEY NOT_BEFORE reject_after` schedules the same
workflow but declares `BatchRejected` after that approved checkpoint. For example:

```sh
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- submit-rejecting batch-rejected 2 44444444-4444-4444-8444-444444444444 0 1
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- approve batch-rejected 0
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- wait RETURNED_UUID 5000
```

Wait prints `REJECTED batch-rejected 1 rejected after acknowledged checkpoint`.
The typed declared error is a canonical durable terminal, not a transport retry.
The approved map entry and acknowledged checkpoint remain committed; workflow
business rejection is not a rollback of previous steps. Further approvals for
the rejected batch are refused, and a new batch may be submitted. RocksDB restart
returns the same typed error without rerunning the rejected body. The generated
handler/Wait error enum distinguishes `BatchRejected` from `Grpc` transport errors;
an arbitrary rich gRPC status does not mint a declared business-terminal receipt.
Public `Checkpoint` RPCs are denied: only dispatcher-owned workflow steps may
advance progress or mark rejection. Only the workflow method declares errors here; declared reader/writer errors in
the same workflow service remain unsupported.


## Atomic transfer between two collections

`client archive batch-001:0000` moves an acknowledged approval entry from the
`approvals` canonical SortedMap into a separately constructed
`archived-approvals` SortedMap, atomically with the app's cumulative `archived`
counter. The RPC returns key/value byte length/counter. Present-empty bytes are
an existing value and transfer normally; absent source or occupied destination
returns typed `ArchiveRejected`, never a false success or overwrite.
`client history batch-001` and `client archive-history batch-001` read each map
through public typed app transactions. Keys retain the existing map constraints.

`client archive-invalid KEY` deliberately catches a declared invalid range after
both map overlays and the tentative counter changed. The whole root must abort:
source removal, destination insertion and counter all roll back. The native
acceptance inspects exact canonical app/map/task/replay records and then verifies
committed transfer plus another transfer after full host/Database restart.
The original completed workflow terminal/checkpoints do not change when the
current app state and collection contents change.

This is a serial handler-awaited fresh exclusive app root with two DISTINCT
same-host map actors; both maps restore ownership before coordinator/task
recovery and readiness. Serial reopening of the same map under this exact root reuses its native
participant. This is not nested/reusable sibling transaction paths, a public/network builtin adapter or distributed placement. Automatic
root idempotency remains disabled for direct-map calls; duplicate transfer
returns source-absent rejection. No status retries or external exactly-once
claim. This acceptance is not a new multi-map lost-ACK/crash-at-Prepare proof.

## Typed workflow-service reader

`work-read batch-001` subscribes once to `Work.ObserveBatch`; `work-unary batch-001`
uses the ordinary reader RPC. `work-mismatch other` returns method-declared
`BatchMismatch` rather than treating a business error as a transport failure.
`watch-work-reconnect` keeps one typed reader client across explicit reconnect,
regeneration and restart; no automatic status retry or durable resume is implied.

The workflow's saved approval observation uses `observe_batch_until`; successful
observations retain existing named replay semantics. A failed observation still
fails/fences the workflow attempt: this is not a saved catchable error decision,
writer-step business-error support or broader workflow error recovery. Changing
saved-step method/request identity for an existing workflow requires a separate
migration; incompatible persisted replay fails closed rather than silently changing
its meaning.

## Catching a saved writer decision

`submit-step-reject step-reject 2 <UUIDv4-key>` selects a workflow which deliberately
tries a rejected `TryCheckpoint` at index zero. Approve index zero normally; the
writer's tentative counter/rejected-state changes are discarded, while its typed
`StepRejected` decision is stored at the named step. The body catches that decision,
executes its ordinary checkpoint and parks for index one. Restart and approve index
one: the same error replays without rerunning the rejected writer, then canonical
Wait returns success. Ordinary Checkpoint and TryCheckpoint RPCs cannot bypass the
private workflow authority.

This is a bounded Rust named-step decision contract, not a claim that Python
implicitly persists aborted writer RPCs. Saved errors require the exact declared
method/type and an otherwise-clean serial attempt; malformed/foreign errors,
framework `Grpc` failures, dropped work and uncertain Store remain fatal. There is
no status-code retry, external-effect exactly-once, cross-actor error recovery or
constructor-error expansion. Ordinary public scheduling-writer errors are transport
responses, not this private saved-decision path.

## Inspect pending tasks

Listing is disabled unless the host receives a nonempty
`RBT_RUST_TASK_ADMIN_TOKEN`. For this isolated development example, choose a
credential privately and export the same value in the host and client terminals
before starting them. Never commit it. Production applications should replace the
example verifier/authorizer with their own identity provider and admin policy.

```sh
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- tasks
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- tasks-watch local-rust 60000
```

The client calls canonical `Tasks.ListTasks` with `only_server_id=local-rust`, a
bearer credential and a deadline. Output columns are UUID, observed phase, method,
phase UTC timestamp, scheduled UTC timestamp (or `-`), task-level completed
iterations and recent local retry failures. Workflow checkpoint indices are not
task-level iterations. A parked approval workflow is `STARTED`; a future task is
`SCHEDULED`. Only framework-owned local retry delays become `SCHEDULED_RETRY`.

This is the singleton dispatcher's current pending-only cache, refreshed by
canonical scans, not an atomic Database snapshot or durable transition history.
Completion disappears on a subsequent scan. Recovery recreates phase timestamps
and resets local failure counts; completed history is not recreated. Listing
checks the original owner generation, uncertainty and accepted placement around
authorization. `tasks-watch` calls canonical `Tasks.ListTasksStream` with the same
explicit server and bearer; the final argument is the RPC deadline in milliseconds
(default 60000). It prints `TASKS count` followed by the same columns for each
initial/changed snapshot. A 200ms pull-owned observation loop rechecks the exact
stream admin policy, original owner generations, activity, uncertainty and placement
before disclosure. No detached polling worker or event queue is created. Slow
consumers coalesce changes; revocation is checked on the next pulled observation,
not pushed independently through transport backpressure. Reconnect after host
restart/regeneration; there is no resume cursor or durable event history.
Cross-server aggregation and shared-reader registry listing are not implemented.
Ordinary RPCs and Wait retain their existing authorization behavior; this admin
policy protects listing, its stream and the bounded cancellation operation below.

## Cancel a scheduled workflow

`client cancel RETURNED_UUID` sends canonical `Tasks.CancelTask` once with the same
admin bearer and a deadline. Only a future-scheduled, not-yet-started workflow is
eligible in the current dispatcher generation. The phase cache is not a durable
never-started certificate across clock rollback/recovery; previous-generation
effects, if any, remain. It persists a canonical system `Cancelled` terminal through completion
CAS before returning `OK`; concurrent/repeated calls or completed/missing tasks
return `NOT_FOUND`. Running/due workflows and ordinary reader/writer task targets
are refused with `FailedPrecondition`, not interrupted. There is no asynchronous
`CANCELLING` state in this bounded contract.

Generated workflow Wait returns the error enum's `Grpc` variant with the exact rich
`Cancelled` status; this example client prints `CANCELLED`. The same terminal survives
RocksDB restart and cannot be reopened by submission replay. This is not business
rejection or rollback: submission state, map entries and earlier effects remain.
In this sample a cancelled batch still occupies its business reservation; a new
submission is rejected. Releasing that reservation needs an application-defined
compensation, not a caller-controlled reset or a fabricated successful workflow.
Transport/ACK uncertainty fails the task owner and requires durable recovery; it
never triggers automatic cancellation retry. General running-task cancellation,
workflow compensation and distributed cancellation remain unsupported.

Local app/map ownership restoration precedes coordinator convergence, stable
EMPTY map constructor replay, task recovery and reactive reader activation.
Stopping/restarting rbt preserves RocksDB in `.rbt/dev/{{ name }}/rust/rocksdb`.
Proto/source edits rebuild the host while retaining Database. Reopen watch after
rebuild. Public mutation commands send once with deadlines; failures may be
uncertain and are not blindly retried. No external-effect exactly-once claim.

```sh
cargo clippy --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --all-targets -- -D warnings
cargo fmt --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml -- --check
cargo test --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --all-targets
```

## Explicit reactive reconnection

`client watch-reconnect [deadline_ms]` retains a single typed query and caller
metadata in one client process. It prints an initial snapshot; enter `next` to
pull a change, `reconnect` to close the old RPC and start a fresh authenticated
scope/baseline, `disconnect` to pause, or `quit` to release it. Reconnect errors
are printed and leave it disconnected. There is no automatic retry by status.

Generated `snapshot` subscriptions expose `disconnect()` and `reconnect()`.
`snapshot_with_timeout(request, duration)` applies one absolute subscription
budget across reads and explicit reconnects; reconnect never resets it.
A successful reconnect emits the current baseline even if unchanged. It may
lose/coalesce intermediate states while disconnected: no durable events, cursor
resume, remote invalidation, or Python React interoperability are claimed.
Server scope/auth is evaluated anew; this does not resurrect revoked authority.

Reactive observation uses `LedgerWorkMethodsReactiveClient::observe` (or
`observe_with_timeout`) on the same workflow-bearing service and handler as the
saved workflow reader step. No separate view service/handler/placement entry is
needed. Only ordinary unary readers are exposed by its local subscription
companion: Create, SubmitBatch, Checkpoint and RunBatch are not subscribe targets.
Private workflow execution/checkpoint authority is unchanged. Transaction-service
subscriptions, mixed workflow/transaction services and declared reader/writer
step errors remain unsupported.

## Reactive approvals on the transaction-bearing service

`LedgerIndexMethods.ApprovalSnapshot` is an ordinary immutable reader on the
same service as `Approve` and `History` transactions. It now has a generated
`LedgerIndexMethodsReactiveClient`; transaction RPCs themselves remain excluded
from subscriptions. Supply a batch name to require it to match the current batch;
a mismatch terminates the subscription with typed `BatchMismatch`, not a guessed
transport status. An empty batch name follows the current batch.

```sh
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- index-read batch-001
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- index-mismatch other
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- watch-index-reconnect 600000
# Interactive commands: next / reconnect / disconnect / quit
```

Explicit reconnect preserves the original query, metadata and absolute deadline.
There are no automatic status retries or distributed invalidation claims. The
host combines workflow and transaction reader bindings behind one local reader
owner for this actor, retaining the original handler/store/auth/transaction
owners. Approval subscribers see acknowledged app/map transaction commits;
failed approvals cannot be used as subscription calls to mutate the ledger.

## Cooperative stop at a durable observation boundary

Use `client submit-stoppable batch 3 <submission-uuid>` for an immediately scheduled
opt-in batch (`submit-stoppable-step-reject` also exercises a saved writer rejection).
Configure a nonempty `RBT_RUST_TASK_ADMIN_TOKEN` on the host and control client,
then send `client stop batch <workflow-uuid> <stable-stop-uuid>`. Without server configuration or a
valid credential, StopBatch denies before handler effects; other development RPCs
retain their existing access rules. Production apps should replace this local
admin verifier with their own identity provider.

The stop request is an ordinary idempotent writer, not a task Cancelled terminal.
It binds the original workflow UUID as well as the batch name; a fresh delayed stop
cannot affect a same-name replacement. Reusing its key with another target is denied.
Its original receipt is replayed for the same key even after completion or a newer
batch; changed requests with the same key collide rather than being reinterpreted.
A parked workflow observes the committed intent without another approval and
saves that exact reader decision. Its private FinishStopped step records the
stopped result; canonical typed Wait returns the original partial counters and
`control true true true` (enabled/requested/stopped). Previously committed work,
approval rows and saved writer-error decisions are retained across restart.
The private completion RPC and reactive subscription to control writers are denied.

New approvals are denied after stop is requested. A unit whose approval was already
durably observed may still finish (including its declared rejection); stopping is
honored at the next observation boundary. Finishing all units first remains normal
completion with `stopped=false`. Thus request acceptance does not promise immediate
or rollback cancellation. After stopped completion a new submission clears control
flags; an old stop-key replay cannot mutate that new batch.

Only immediately scheduled opt-in batches support this control. Delayed opt-in
submission is rejected before staging; existing scheduled-workflow cancellation
remains separate. This demonstrates finite same-actor saved-decision branching,
not Python unbounded cursor/Break/GC parity, running/distributed task cancellation,
or external-effects exactly-once semantics. Ordinary batches keep their existing
condition identity and behavior; opt-in waits use `approved-or-stop.v1`.

## Atomic multi-entry archive

`client archive-many batch-001:0000,batch-001:0001` transfers 1..100 unique,
nonempty keys in one exclusive app transaction. Each entry reopens the two map
sessions under the same root and preserves present-empty byte values. All source
removals, archive insertions and cumulative counter changes commit together.
A missing later source or occupied destination aborts earlier transfers too;
there is no per-entry commit or compensating rollback. `archive-many-invalid`
exercises caught invalid range failure after the final transfer and must abort
all participants.

This reuses the exact root-owned same-host map incarnation, not nested child
transaction snapshots, distributed siblings or a public map adapter. Commands
send once without root idempotency or status retry; an uncertain result is not
permission to send it again. Restart restores app and both map owners before
coordinator/task recovery and public readiness.

## Task result access

Wait remains public for local development unless `RBT_RUST_TASK_RESULT_GRANT`
is configured on the host. This opt-in file contains JSON `token` and `task_uuid`
strings; create it outside the repository with private permissions and use a
fresh credential. Canonical and generated Wait calls must provide that bearer
credential and the granted task UUID. Task-admin credentials are independent.
Missing, malformed or revoked grants fail closed; the host rechecks before and
after canonical task loads and while a pending Wait polls. Revoking the file
ends pending Wait without cancelling or changing the task. Restore the grant
to retrieve its durable result, including after host/Database restart.

This is a local-development policy example, not an identity provider, automatic
task-creator ACL, Python middleware equivalence or an atomic policy-revocation
transaction. Production applications supply their own verifier/authorizer through
`ReaderTaskWaitService::with_wait_authorization`. Policies receive the exact
encoded Wait request and actor identity, not the actor state or task result.

## Live paginated history export

`client history-page false 25 false - - -` reads the approval index. Use `true`
for the archive index. Arguments are archive, page size (1..100), reverse,
optional inclusive start, optional exclusive end, and optional continuation;
`-` means absent. The JSON response contains ordered key/value entries and a
nullable continuation. Repeat the original map/bounds/direction with that token;
page size may change. Forward bounds increase, reverse bounds decrease.
Unlike the legacy batch history command's 100-key cap, unbounded key ranges can
browse every batch through bounded pages. Empty values remain byte arrays.

Continuation uses the first unreturned key, not a fabricated key successor.
It is versioned query data bound to map identity, original bounds and direction,
not a credential or authorization token. Malformed/foreign-query tokens fail.
This is **live keyset traversal, not a snapshot across RPCs**: changes between
pages can appear or disappear; inserts before the resume key are not revisited.
An unchanged dataset can resume after host/Database restart. Each page uses the
existing serial exclusive app/map transaction and sends once; no automatic root
idempotency, status retry, distributed map ingress or uncertain-outcome retry is
introduced.
